(window.__PQ=window.__PQ||{})["06-cicd"]={"slug":"06-cicd","title":"CI/CD","accent":"#fb923c","sections":[{"id":"s01-cicd-concepts","title":"CI/CD concepts and pipeline design","c":[{"id":"s01-cicd-concepts-c1","t":"Continuous integration","p":"Continuous integration (CI) means every developer merges small changes into the shared branch often, and every change is built and tested automatically by a server within minutes.","k":[["What is the one rule that makes CI work?","The shared branch is merged into often and every merge is verified automatically, so it never stays broken for long."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s01-cicd-concepts-c2","t":"Continuous delivery versus continuous deployment","p":"Continuous delivery means every change that passes the pipeline is ready to release at the push of a button. Continuous deployment goes one step further and releases it automatically with no human click.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s01-cicd-concepts-c3","t":"Stages of a pipeline","p":"A pipeline is a list of stages, and each stage is a group of jobs that must pass before the next group starts. A common order is build, test, scan, package, deploy and verify.","k":[["Why run lint before integration tests?","It fails in seconds, so developers learn about trivial problems without waiting for slow stages."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s01-cicd-concepts-c4","t":"Fast feedback and the test pyramid","p":"The test pyramid says write many fast unit tests, fewer integration tests and very few end to end tests. Pipelines should be ordered the same way so the quickest signal arrives first.","k":[["What should you do about a flaky test?","Quarantine or fix it quickly. Retrying hides the problem and trains people to distrust the pipeline."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s01-cicd-concepts-c5","t":"Build once, deploy many","p":"Build the artifact a single time, give it an immutable identity such as a digest, and move that exact artifact through dev, staging and production. Only configuration differs between environments.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s01-cicd-concepts-c6","t":"Pipeline as code","p":"The pipeline definition lives in the same repository as the application, in a text file such as .github/workflows/.yml, Jenkinsfile or .gitlab-ci.yml, and is reviewed like any other code.","k":[["Why keep pipeline files next to the code?","They are versioned, reviewed and branched together with the application they build."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s01-cicd-concepts-c7","t":"Environments and promotion","p":"An environment is a place where the service runs, such as dev, staging and production. Promotion is moving a tested artifact from one to the next, with rising confidence at each step.","k":[["What should differ between staging and production?","Scale, secrets and endpoints. The image and the deployment method should be identical."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s02-branching-release-flows","title":"Trunk-based development and release flows","c":[{"id":"s02-branching-release-flows-c1","t":"Trunk-based development","p":"Everyone commits to one main branch (the trunk), either directly or through very short-lived branches that last hours or a day or two. Unfinished work is hidden behind feature flags.","k":[["How can unfinished work live on main safely?","Behind a feature flag that is off, so the code is deployed but not active."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s02-branching-release-flows-c2","t":"GitHub flow","p":"GitHub flow is a light version of trunk-based work. Branch from main, open a pull request, get review and green checks, merge, and deploy from main.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s02-branching-release-flows-c3","t":"Gitflow and release branches","p":"Gitflow uses long-lived develop and main branches plus feature, release and hotfix branches. It suits software shipped in versions that several supported releases must coexist.","k":[["When is a release branch justified?","When you must keep supporting an older version while developing the next one."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s02-branching-release-flows-c4","t":"Required checks and branch protection","p":"Branch protection lets the platform refuse a merge until named checks pass and reviewers approve. It turns the pipeline from advice into a gate.","k":[["Why mark a check as required rather than just visible?","Only required checks block the merge button."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s02-branching-release-flows-c5","t":"Merge queues","p":"A merge queue tests each pull request combined with the ones ahead of it before merging, so main never receives a combination that was not tested.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s02-branching-release-flows-c6","t":"Versioning and tagging","p":"A version number and a tag give a release a name that humans and machines can refer to. Semantic versioning uses MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH, where MAJOR breaks compatibility, MINOR adds features and PATCH fixes bugs.","k":[["What should an image tag tell you?","Which commit and which version it was built from, so production can be traced to source."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s02-branching-release-flows-c7","t":"Monorepo and polyrepo pipelines","p":"A monorepo holds many services in one repository, a polyrepo gives each service its own. Each choice changes how pipelines decide what to build.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s03-gha-fundamentals","title":"GitHub Actions fundamentals","c":[{"id":"s03-gha-fundamentals-c1","t":"Workflows, jobs and steps","p":"A workflow is one YAML file. It contains jobs, and each job contains steps. Jobs run on separate machines at the same time unless you order them. Steps inside a job run in sequence on the same machine and share its filesystem.","k":[["Do two jobs share files?","No. Each job gets a clean runner. Use artifacts or caches to pass data."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s03-gha-fundamentals-c2","t":"Events and triggers","p":"The on: key says what starts a workflow: a push, a pull request, a schedule, a manual click, a call from another workflow, or an external webhook.","k":[["Which branch does a schedule trigger run on?","The default branch."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s03-gha-fundamentals-c3","t":"Runners","p":"A runner is the machine that executes a job. GitHub-hosted runners are fresh virtual machines (Ubuntu, Windows, macOS) created for each job. Self-hosted runners are machines you operate yourself.","k":[["Why prefer ephemeral runners?","Each job starts clean, so one job cannot leave files or credentials for the next."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s03-gha-fundamentals-c4","t":"Contexts, expressions and conditions","p":"Inside ${{ }} you can read data about the run (contexts such as github, env, secrets, matrix, needs) and use functions and operators to decide what happens.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s03-gha-fundamentals-c5","t":"Matrix builds","p":"A matrix runs the same job many times with different values, such as several language versions or operating systems. GitHub creates one job per combination.","k":[["How do you stop one failure from cancelling the others?","Set fail-fast to false under strategy."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s03-gha-fundamentals-c6","t":"Dependency caching","p":"The cache action stores files such as downloaded packages under a key, and restores them in a later run when the key matches.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s03-gha-fundamentals-c7","t":"Artifacts","p":"An artifact is a file or folder that a job uploads to the workflow run so that later jobs, or people, can download it. Examples are test reports, binaries and coverage files.","k":[["When do you use cache and when artifact?","Cache speeds up inputs like dependencies. Artifacts carry outputs of the run between jobs or to people."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s04-gha-reuse-control","title":"GitHub Actions reuse and flow control","c":[{"id":"s04-gha-reuse-control-c1","t":"Reusable workflows","p":"A reusable workflow is a complete workflow that other workflows call as a job, using on: workflowcall. It can take inputs and secrets and return outputs.","k":[["Where do you write `uses` for a reusable workflow?","At job level, in place of `runs-on` and `steps`."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s04-gha-reuse-control-c2","t":"Composite actions","p":"A composite action bundles several steps into one reusable step. It lives in a folder with an action.yml file and is called with uses: inside a job.","k":[["What must every run step in a composite action declare?","The shell."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s04-gha-reuse-control-c3","t":"Environments and approvals","p":"A GitHub environment such as production is a named target with its own rules, secrets and variables. A job with environment: production waits for those rules.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s04-gha-reuse-control-c4","t":"Concurrency control","p":"The concurrency key gives runs a group name. Only one run per group is active at a time, and you choose whether the older run is cancelled or the new one waits.","k":[["Why not cancel an in-progress deployment?","It could stop halfway and leave the environment in a mixed state."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s04-gha-reuse-control-c5","t":"Variables, secrets and configuration","p":"Variables (vars) hold plain configuration. Secrets hold sensitive values and are masked in logs. Both can be set per repository, per environment or per organisation.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s04-gha-reuse-control-c6","t":"Workflow templates and organisation defaults","p":"A repository named .github in your organisation can hold starter workflows and default community files, so new repositories begin from approved patterns.","k":[["How do actions pinned by SHA stay current?","A tool such as Dependabot opens pull requests that update the pinned SHA and its version comment."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s04-gha-reuse-control-c7","t":"Debugging workflows","p":"Most failures are YAML mistakes, wrong expressions, missing permissions or environment differences. A short set of habits finds them quickly.","k":[["Which tool checks workflow syntax before pushing?","actionlint."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s05-gha-security-oidc","title":"GitHub Actions security and OIDC to AWS","c":[{"id":"s05-gha-security-oidc-c1","t":"The GITHUB_TOKEN and permissions","p":"Every run gets an automatic token called GITHUBTOKEN. The permissions: key sets exactly what that token may do, such as read contents or write packages.","k":[["What happens to unlisted permissions when you set some?","They become none."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s05-gha-security-oidc-c2","t":"OIDC federation to AWS","p":"Instead of storing an AWS access key in GitHub, the workflow asks GitHub for a short-lived signed identity token (a JWT). AWS verifies it and hands back temporary credentials for an IAM role.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s05-gha-security-oidc-c3","t":"Choosing the sub claim","p":"The sub claim describes which workflow context is asking. Its format depends on the trigger: branch, tag, pull request or environment. The trust policy matches on it.","k":[["Which sub form do you use to bind a role to an approval gate?","The environment form, repo:ORG/REPO:environment:NAME."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s05-gha-security-oidc-c4","t":"Reaching Kubernetes on EKS from a workflow","p":"After the workflow assumes an AWS role, it can ask EKS for a cluster token. The IAM role is mapped to Kubernetes RBAC with EKS access entries (or the older aws-auth ConfigMap).","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s05-gha-security-oidc-c5","t":"Third-party actions and supply chain risk","p":"An action is code that runs inside your job with your token and secrets. Using uses: someone/action@v1 means trusting that person, and everyone who can move the v1 tag.","k":[["Why is a tag not safe for third-party code?","Tags can be moved to different code by whoever controls the repository."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s05-gha-security-oidc-c6","t":"Script injection and pull_request_target","p":"Expressions are replaced by text before the shell runs. If the text comes from an attacker (a branch name, issue title or PR body), it becomes shell code. pullrequesttarget runs with secrets in the context of the base branch, so checking out fork code there is dangerous.","k":[["Why does env: protect against injection?","The value is set as a variable, not pasted into the script text, so the shell treats it as data."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s05-gha-security-oidc-c7","t":"Auditing workflows","p":"Static analysis tools read your workflow files and report dangerous patterns, such as unpinned actions, excessive permissions and injectable expressions.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s06-gha-runners-arc","title":"Self-hosted runners and Actions Runner Controller","c":[{"id":"s06-gha-runners-arc-c1","t":"When to self-host","p":"Self-hosting means you run the runner agent on machines you manage. It connects out to GitHub, picks up jobs and reports results.","k":[["Why are public forks a danger for self-hosted runners?","Pull request code from anyone could run on a machine inside your network."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s06-gha-runners-arc-c2","t":"Ephemeral runners","p":"An ephemeral runner accepts exactly one job and then unregisters and disappears. A new clean one is created for the next job.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s06-gha-runners-arc-c3","t":"Actions Runner Controller on Kubernetes","p":"ARC is a Kubernetes operator from GitHub that creates runner pods when jobs are waiting and removes them afterwards. It uses runner scale sets.","k":[["What does a workflow put in runs-on to use an ARC scale set?","The scale set installation name."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s06-gha-runners-arc-c4","t":"ARC configuration and authentication","p":"ARC needs credentials to talk to GitHub. A GitHub App is preferred to a personal access token for organisations. The runner pod spec is customised through Helm values.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s06-gha-runners-arc-c5","t":"Building images without a privileged daemon","p":"Running docker build inside a pod normally needs a Docker daemon and elevated privileges. Daemonless builders such as BuildKit rootless, Kaniko-style tools or remote builders avoid mounting the host socket.","k":[["Why avoid mounting the Docker socket into CI pods?","The job could then start privileged containers on the node and escape."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s06-gha-runners-arc-c6","t":"Hardening runners","p":"A runner holds credentials and executes arbitrary code from the repository. Hardening limits what a malicious job can do and what it can reach.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s06-gha-runners-arc-c7","t":"Cost and capacity of runners","p":"Hosted runners bill by the minute, self-hosted cost compute and people time. Knowing your minutes and queue time shows which is cheaper.","k":[["What is the first step before self-hosting to save money?","Measure where minutes go and cut waste with caching, filters and cancellation."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s07-jenkins-architecture","title":"Jenkins architecture","c":[{"id":"s07-jenkins-architecture-c1","t":"Controller and agents","p":"The controller (formerly master) hosts the web UI, stores configuration and schedules builds. Agents are the machines or pods that actually run the build steps.","k":[["Why set the controller to zero executors?","Builds then cannot run on it, protecting its credentials and keeping the scheduler responsive."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s07-jenkins-architecture-c2","t":"Executors, queue and workspaces","p":"An executor is one build slot on a node. The queue holds builds waiting for a free slot with a matching label. Each build gets a workspace, a directory where its files live.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s07-jenkins-architecture-c3","t":"Job types","p":"Jenkins offers freestyle jobs (configured in the UI), Pipeline jobs (a script), Multibranch pipelines (one job per branch or pull request found in a repository) and organisation folders (scan a whole GitHub or GitLab organisation).","k":[["What does a multibranch pipeline do when a branch is deleted?","It removes the branch job according to the orphaned item strategy."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s07-jenkins-architecture-c4","t":"Triggering builds","p":"Builds start by webhook from Git, by a timer (cron syntax), by another job, or by a person. Webhooks give the fastest and cheapest result.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s07-jenkins-architecture-c5","t":"Installing on Kubernetes","p":"The official Helm chart deploys the controller as a StatefulSet-like workload with a persistent volume for JENKINSHOME. Agents are created as separate pods by the Kubernetes plugin.","k":[["Can you run two active Jenkins controller replicas for scale?","No. The open source controller is a single active instance. Scale by adding agents or splitting into several controllers."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s07-jenkins-architecture-c6","t":"Authentication and authorization","p":"Jenkins needs a security realm (who are you: local users, LDAP, OIDC or SAML) and an authorization strategy (what may you do).","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s07-jenkins-architecture-c7","t":"Pipeline execution and durability","p":"A Pipeline run is executed by the Groovy CPS engine on the controller, while steps such as sh run on agents. State is saved to disk so a run can survive a controller restart.","k":[["Where does Groovy pipeline code execute?","On the controller, which is why heavy computation there harms everyone."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s08-jenkinsfile","title":"Jenkinsfile declarative and scripted","c":[{"id":"s08-jenkinsfile-c1","t":"Declarative pipeline structure","p":"A declarative Jenkinsfile has a fixed shape: pipeline { agent ... stages { stage ... steps ... } post ... }. The structure is validated before the build starts.","k":[["When is a declarative Jenkinsfile validated?","Before the run starts, so structural errors fail immediately."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s08-jenkinsfile-c2","t":"Stages, parallel and when","p":"Stages run in order. parallel runs several branches at once, and when decides whether a stage runs at all.","k":[["What does beforeAgent do?","It evaluates the when condition before allocating an agent, saving resources for skipped stages."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s08-jenkinsfile-c3","t":"Parameters, input and approvals","p":"parameters lets a person fill in values when starting a run. The input step pauses the pipeline until someone approves, optionally with a value.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s08-jenkinsfile-c4","t":"Post actions and notifications","p":"The post section runs after a stage or the whole pipeline, depending on outcome: always, success, failure, unstable, aborted, changed, cleanup.","k":[["Which post condition runs even when other post conditions fail?","cleanup."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s08-jenkinsfile-c5","t":"Scripted pipeline","p":"Scripted pipeline is plain Groovy with node { stage(...) { ... } }. It has no fixed structure and gives full programming freedom.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s08-jenkinsfile-c6","t":"Stash, artifacts and test reports","p":"stash saves files from one stage for use in another stage on a different agent. archiveArtifacts keeps files with the build. junit publishes test results to the UI.","k":[["Why not stash a multi-gigabyte image?","Stashes travel through the controller and are meant for small files. Push big artifacts to a registry or object storage."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s08-jenkinsfile-c7","t":"Linting and testing Jenkinsfiles","p":"You can validate a Jenkinsfile without running it, and replay or test pipeline code against a real controller.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s09-jenkins-libs-k8s-ops","title":"Jenkins libraries, Kubernetes agents and operations","c":[{"id":"s09-jenkins-libs-k8s-ops-c1","t":"Shared libraries","p":"A shared library is a Git repository of Groovy code that pipelines import. It holds common steps so teams do not copy pipeline logic.","k":[["Where do global steps live in a library?","In the vars directory, one file per step with a call method."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s09-jenkins-libs-k8s-ops-c2","t":"Kubernetes pod agents","p":"With the Kubernetes plugin, each build creates a pod with the containers it needs, runs the steps and deletes the pod. A pod template describes the containers.","k":[["How do you run a step in a specific tool container of the pod?","Wrap it in container('name') { ... }."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s09-jenkins-libs-k8s-ops-c3","t":"Credentials in Jenkins","p":"The credentials store keeps secrets (text, username and password, SSH keys, files, certificates) encrypted on the controller. Pipelines request them by ID and the plugin masks them in logs.","k":[["Why use single quotes with secrets in sh?","Groovy would otherwise expand the secret into the command text before the shell runs, exposing it in logs and processes."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s09-jenkins-libs-k8s-ops-c4","t":"Configuration as code","p":"The Configuration as Code plugin (JCasC) describes the whole controller configuration in YAML: security, agents, credentials, tools and global settings.","k":[["Where do secrets in JCasC come from?","Environment variables or files mounted from a Kubernetes secret, referenced as variables in the YAML."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s09-jenkins-libs-k8s-ops-c5","t":"Plugin hygiene","p":"Plugins add features and are also the main source of Jenkins security advisories. Keeping a short, pinned, regularly updated list is core maintenance.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s09-jenkins-libs-k8s-ops-c6","t":"Scaling and performance","p":"One controller has limits on memory, build history and concurrent builds. You scale by moving work to agents, reducing controller load, and if needed splitting into several controllers.","k":[["What is the first scaling step for Jenkins?","Move all builds to agents and reduce controller load, before considering more controllers."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s09-jenkins-libs-k8s-ops-c7","t":"Backup and disaster recovery","p":"The controller keeps state in JENKINSHOME. A backup plan means you can restore job history, credentials and configuration after a failure.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s10-gitlab-circleci-tekton","title":"GitLab CI and other CI systems","c":[{"id":"s10-gitlab-circleci-tekton-c1","t":"GitLab CI pipeline basics","p":"GitLab CI reads .gitlab-ci.yml at the repository root. The file defines stages (ordered phases) and jobs (units of work that belong to a stage). Jobs in the same stage run in parallel.","k":[["What does needs: [] do?","The job has no dependencies and starts immediately, ignoring stage order."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s10-gitlab-circleci-tekton-c2","t":"Rules, workflow and conditions","p":"rules: decides whether a job is added to the pipeline, using conditions on variables, changed files and the pipeline source. workflow:rules does the same for the whole pipeline.","k":[["What happens to a job when no rule matches?","It is not added to the pipeline."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s10-gitlab-circleci-tekton-c3","t":"GitLab runners and executors","p":"A GitLab Runner is the agent that runs jobs. The executor decides how: shell, Docker, or Kubernetes (a pod per job). Runners are shared across a group or dedicated to a project, and are selected by tags.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s10-gitlab-circleci-tekton-c4","t":"Includes, templates and components","p":"include: pulls configuration from other files, another project, a remote URL or a template. extends: and YAML anchors reuse job fragments. CI/CD components are versioned, parameterised building blocks published in a catalog.","k":[["What does the leading dot in a job name mean?","The job is hidden and never runs on its own, so it can serve as a template."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s10-gitlab-circleci-tekton-c5","t":"GitLab environments, review apps and deployments","p":"An environment: on a job records deployments in GitLab with a URL, history and optional rollback. Review apps deploy every merge request to a temporary environment.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s10-gitlab-circleci-tekton-c6","t":"CircleCI","p":"CircleCI is a hosted CI service configured in .circleci/config.yml. Work is organised as jobs that run in an executor (Docker image, machine or macOS) and workflows that order them. Reusable packages are called orbs.","k":[["What does a CircleCI workflow add on top of jobs?","The ordering and dependencies between jobs, using requires."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s10-gitlab-circleci-tekton-c7","t":"Tekton and Argo Workflows","p":"Tekton and Argo Workflows are Kubernetes-native engines. Their pipelines are Kubernetes custom resources, and each step runs as a container in a pod on your cluster.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s11-choosing-ci-system","title":"Choosing a CI/CD system","c":[{"id":"s11-choosing-ci-system-c1","t":"The decision matrix","p":"A matrix scores each tool on the criteria that matter to you. This one summarises typical strengths. Treat entries as general tendencies and verify against current product docs and your plan.","k":[["Why should the matrix weights come first?","Otherwise the scores reflect preference rather than the constraints you actually have."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s11-choosing-ci-system-c2","t":"Hosting model and operating effort","p":"Hosted services take away servers and upgrades but bring cost per minute and less network control. Self-managed systems give control at the price of operating them.","k":[["What does the hybrid model give you?","Private network access with the vendor handling the control plane."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s11-choosing-ci-system-c3","t":"Ecosystem, lock-in and portability","p":"Every tool has its own YAML or Groovy dialect. Moving between them is costly if all logic lives in that dialect.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s11-choosing-ci-system-c4","t":"Security and compliance fit","p":"Different systems offer different built-in controls: audit logs, protected environments, approvals, secrets management and SSO. Match them to your compliance obligations.","k":[["Which feature binds a production deploy to an approval?","Environments with required reviewers in GitHub, protected environments in GitLab, or input steps in Jenkins."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s11-choosing-ci-system-c5","t":"Cost models","p":"Hosted tools charge per minute, per credit or per seat. Self-managed tools charge for compute and for people. The cheaper option depends on volume and utilisation.","k":[["What cost is often forgotten for self-hosting?","Operator time, idle capacity and developer wait time."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s11-choosing-ci-system-c6","t":"A pragmatic recommendation","p":"For a team on GitHub that deploys to EKS, the common modern pattern is GitHub Actions for CI, runners in the VPC if private access is needed, OIDC to AWS, and Argo CD or Flux for deployment.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s11-choosing-ci-system-c7","t":"Migrating between systems","p":"Migrate in slices, not all at once. Run the old and new pipelines side by side, compare results, then cut over service by service.","k":[["What is the safest way to switch a service to the new CI?","Run both pipelines side by side, compare, then cut over and retire the old one."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s12-argocd-core","title":"GitOps with Argo CD","c":[{"id":"s12-argocd-core-c1","t":"The GitOps model","p":"In GitOps, a Git repository describes what should run. An agent inside the cluster pulls that description and applies it continuously, instead of a pipeline pushing changes from outside.","k":[["Where do cluster credentials live in pull-based GitOps?","Inside the cluster. CI never holds them."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s12-argocd-core-c2","t":"Argo CD architecture and install","p":"Argo CD runs as a set of components in the argocd namespace: an API server (UI and CLI), a repo server that renders manifests, an application controller that compares and syncs, and Redis for caching.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s12-argocd-core-c3","t":"The Application resource","p":"An Application custom resource links one source (a path in a Git repository, or a Helm chart) to one destination (a cluster and namespace).","k":[["What does targetRevision control?","Which branch, tag or commit of the repository Argo CD tracks."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s12-argocd-core-c4","t":"Sync policies and options","p":"Sync means applying the Git state to the cluster. It can be manual (someone clicks sync) or automated, with extra switches that decide how aggressive it is.","k":[["What does selfHeal do?","It reverts changes made directly in the cluster so it matches Git again."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s12-argocd-core-c5","t":"Sync waves and hooks","p":"Resources can be given an order with a sync wave annotation, and jobs can run at specific moments with hooks such as PreSync and PostSync.","k":[["Which number runs first, wave 2 or wave -1?","-1. Lower numbers run first."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s12-argocd-core-c6","t":"Health assessment and sync status","p":"Argo CD shows two separate states. Sync status says whether live matches Git (Synced or OutOfSync). Health says whether the resources work (Healthy, Progressing, Degraded, Missing, Suspended).","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s12-argocd-core-c7","t":"Drift detection and self-heal","p":"Drift is any difference between Git and the cluster, such as a manual kubectl edit. Argo CD compares continuously, marks the app OutOfSync, and can revert the change with self-heal.","k":[["How do you do an emergency change without fighting self-heal?","Temporarily disable auto-sync, apply the change, commit it to Git and re-enable."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s12-argocd-core-c8","t":"Repository layout and manifests","p":"Keep application code and deployment configuration in separate repositories, or at least separate directories, with an overlay per environment.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s13-argocd-scale","title":"Argo CD at scale","c":[{"id":"s13-argocd-scale-c1","t":"ApplicationSet","p":"An ApplicationSet is a template plus generators. The generators produce a list of parameter sets, and the controller creates one Application for each.","k":[["What does the Git directory generator produce per directory?","One set of parameters, so one Application per matching directory."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s13-argocd-scale-c2","t":"App of apps","p":"The app-of-apps pattern uses one root Application whose source directory contains other Application manifests. Syncing the root creates all the child apps.","k":[["What is created when the root app syncs?","The child Application resources it contains, which in turn deploy their own sources."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s13-argocd-scale-c3","t":"Multi-cluster management","p":"One Argo CD can deploy to many clusters. You register each target cluster, which stores a credential as a Secret, and then set destination.server or destination.name.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s13-argocd-scale-c4","t":"AppProjects","p":"An AppProject limits which repositories an application may use, which clusters and namespaces it may deploy to, and which resource kinds it may create.","k":[["What does an empty clusterResourceWhitelist do?","It permits no cluster-scoped resources, so the team cannot create things like ClusterRoles."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s13-argocd-scale-c5","t":"RBAC in Argo CD","p":"Argo CD has its own RBAC, separate from Kubernetes. Policies in the argocd-rbac-cm ConfigMap say which users or groups may get, sync, override or delete which applications.","k":[["Who can sync production in the policy above?","Only role admin. shop-dev may sync apps ending in -staging."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s13-argocd-scale-c6","t":"SSO with OIDC","p":"Argo CD can log users in through OIDC (directly or through Dex). Your identity provider supplies the user and group claims that the RBAC policy uses.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s13-argocd-scale-c7","t":"Secrets in GitOps","p":"Git cannot hold plain Kubernetes Secrets. Instead you commit an encrypted form or a reference, and a controller turns it into a Secret in the cluster.","k":[["What does Git store when using External Secrets?","Only a reference to the secret in the external store, not the value."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s14-flux","title":"GitOps with Flux","c":[{"id":"s14-flux-c1","t":"Flux architecture and bootstrap","p":"Flux runs in the flux-system namespace as separate controllers: source, kustomize, helm and notification, plus optional image automation. flux bootstrap installs them and commits their own manifests to Git.","k":[["Where does flux bootstrap put Flux's own manifests?","In the Git repository at the path you gave, so Flux manages itself from Git."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s14-flux-c2","t":"GitRepository and sources","p":"A GitRepository object tells source-controller which repository and branch to fetch and how often. Other resources then refer to it as their source.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s14-flux-c3","t":"Flux Kustomization","p":"A Flux Kustomization points at a path inside a source and applies it, like an Argo CD Application. It adds pruning, health checks, dependencies and variable substitution.","k":[["What does dependsOn guarantee?","The dependency is applied and ready before this Kustomization is reconciled."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s14-flux-c4","t":"HelmRelease","p":"A HelmRelease installs and upgrades a Helm chart declaratively. Charts come from a HelmRepository, a GitRepository or an OCIRepository.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s14-flux-c5","t":"Image automation","p":"Flux can watch a container registry for new tags, choose the newest that matches a policy, and commit the new tag back to Git. Three resources do this: ImageRepository, ImagePolicy and ImageUpdateAutomation.","k":[["Which resource decides which tag is newest?","The ImagePolicy."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s14-flux-c6","t":"Flux operations and troubleshooting","p":"The flux CLI shows the state of every object, forces a reconcile, suspends changes and traces which object created a resource.","k":[["How do you pause Flux for one Kustomization?","flux suspend kustomization NAME, then flux resume when done."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s14-flux-c7","t":"Flux versus Argo CD","p":"Both implement GitOps well. Argo CD gives a rich UI and an application model. Flux gives composable controllers and tight Helm and Kustomize integration, without a central UI by default.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s15-artifacts-supply-chain","title":"Artifacts, registries and supply chain security","c":[{"id":"s15-artifacts-supply-chain-c1","t":"Artifacts and registries","p":"An artifact is any output you deploy or depend on: container images, Helm charts, language packages, binaries. A registry is the versioned store where they live.","k":[["What is the difference between a tag and a digest?","A tag is a movable label. A digest is the hash of the content and cannot change."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s15-artifacts-supply-chain-c2","t":"ECR configuration for CI","p":"Amazon ECR is the registry you will usually use on EKS. A few settings make it safe: immutable tags, scan on push, encryption and lifecycle policies.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s15-artifacts-supply-chain-c3","t":"Software bill of materials","p":"An SBOM is a machine-readable list of everything inside an artifact: packages, versions and licences. The two common formats are SPDX and CycloneDX.","k":[["Why keep the SBOM instead of regenerating it?","It records exactly what shipped at build time and can be queried when new vulnerabilities appear, without access to the image."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s15-artifacts-supply-chain-c4","t":"Signing images with cosign","p":"Signing attaches a cryptographic proof that a specific digest was published by a specific identity. cosign from the Sigstore project signs and verifies OCI artifacts.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s15-artifacts-supply-chain-c5","t":"Provenance and SLSA","p":"Provenance is a signed record of how an artifact was built: which source commit, which builder, which inputs. SLSA (Supply-chain Levels for Software Artifacts) defines levels of assurance for that process.","k":[["What does provenance prove that a signature alone does not?","How and from which source the artifact was built, not only who published it."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s15-artifacts-supply-chain-c6","t":"Attestations","p":"An attestation is a signed statement about an artifact, such as \"this image was scanned and had no critical findings\", \"this is its SBOM\", or \"this is its provenance\". It is attached to the image digest.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s15-artifacts-supply-chain-c7","t":"Admission verification in the cluster","p":"A policy engine running as an admission controller can refuse to create pods whose images are not signed by your CI identity, or lack required attestations.","k":[["Why start in audit mode?","It shows which existing workloads would be blocked, avoiding outages when enforcement begins."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s15-artifacts-supply-chain-c8","t":"Dependency and base image hygiene","p":"Most vulnerabilities in your service arrive through dependencies and the base image. Pinning, updating and minimising them reduces what you must patch.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s16-deployment-strategies","title":"Deployment strategies and progressive delivery","c":[{"id":"s16-deployment-strategies-c1","t":"Rolling updates","p":"A Kubernetes Deployment replaces pods gradually. New pods start, pass readiness, and then old pods are removed, so the service stays available.","k":[["Which setting keeps full capacity during an update?","maxUnavailable set to 0, with maxSurge at least 1."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s16-deployment-strategies-c2","t":"Blue-green deployments","p":"Run two complete environments: blue is live, green has the new version. When green passes checks, switch traffic to it in one step. Blue stays for instant rollback.","k":[["What is the main advantage over rolling updates?","Instant, clean cutover and rollback with no mixed versions serving at once."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s16-deployment-strategies-c3","t":"Canary releases","p":"A canary sends a small share of traffic, for example 5 percent, to the new version, watches metrics, and increases the share only if the version behaves well.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s16-deployment-strategies-c4","t":"Argo Rollouts","p":"Argo Rollouts adds a Rollout resource, a replacement for Deployment, with built-in canary and blue-green strategies, traffic routing and automated analysis.","k":[["What happens if the analysis fails?","The rollout aborts and traffic goes back to the stable version."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s16-deployment-strategies-c5","t":"Flagger","p":"Flagger is a progressive delivery operator from the Flux project. You keep your normal Deployment and add a Canary resource. Flagger creates the primary and canary copies and moves traffic by calling your mesh or ingress.","k":[["Which object do you keep with Flagger that Argo Rollouts replaces?","The normal Deployment. Flagger wraps it, while Argo Rollouts replaces it with a Rollout."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s16-deployment-strategies-c6","t":"Feature flags","p":"A feature flag is a runtime switch that turns code on or off without a deploy. It separates deploying code from releasing a feature.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s16-deployment-strategies-c7","t":"Database migrations","p":"Schema changes must work with both the old and new application version, because during any rollout both run at once. The safe method is expand and contract.","k":[["Why not drop a column in the same release that stops using it?","During and after rollback the old version still runs and needs the column."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s16-deployment-strategies-c8","t":"Rollbacks and automatic recovery","p":"Rollback means returning to the last known good version quickly. In GitOps, that is a revert commit. With progressive delivery, it can be automatic.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s17-pipeline-security","title":"Pipeline security and secrets","c":[{"id":"s17-pipeline-security-c1","t":"Threats to a pipeline","p":"Attackers target pipelines to steal secrets, push malicious code to artifacts, or use the runner as a foothold in your network.","k":[["What mix makes pipelines attractive targets?","Untrusted input combined with powerful credentials."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s17-pipeline-security-c2","t":"Least privilege for pipeline identities","p":"Give each pipeline job only the permissions it needs, for as short a time as possible, using separate identities for separate stages.","k":[["Why split the build and deploy roles?","A compromised build then cannot touch the cluster, and a deploy job cannot rewrite images."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s17-pipeline-security-c3","t":"Secrets management in CI","p":"The best secret is the one you do not have. Prefer short-lived identity (OIDC) over stored keys. Where a secret is required, keep it in a dedicated store and fetch it at the moment of use.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s17-pipeline-security-c4","t":"Secret detection","p":"Secret scanners search code and Git history for keys, tokens and passwords. They run before commit, on pull requests and across history.","k":[["What is the first action after a secret leaks?","Revoke and rotate it. Cleaning history comes after."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s17-pipeline-security-c5","t":"SAST and SCA","p":"SAST (static application security testing) analyses your source for insecure patterns. SCA (software composition analysis) checks your dependencies against vulnerability databases and licence rules.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s17-pipeline-security-c6","t":"Container and IaC scanning","p":"Scan the built image for vulnerable OS packages and libraries, and scan Dockerfiles, Kubernetes manifests, Helm charts and Terraform for misconfiguration before they are applied.","k":[["Why re-scan images that already passed?","New vulnerabilities are published after the build."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s17-pipeline-security-c7","t":"DAST and runtime checks","p":"DAST (dynamic application security testing) attacks a running test deployment from outside, like a scanner or attacker would. It finds problems that only appear at runtime, such as missing headers or exposed endpoints.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s17-pipeline-security-c8","t":"Protecting the pipeline definition","p":"Anyone who can change the pipeline file can run code with its permissions. Treat changes to workflows, Jenkinsfiles and deployment repositories as security-sensitive.","k":[["Why require review for workflow file changes specifically?","The workflow runs with the pipeline permissions, so editing it can reach secrets and deploy rights."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s18-performance-dora-worked-eks","title":"Pipeline performance, DORA metrics and a worked EKS pipeline","c":[{"id":"s18-performance-dora-worked-eks-c1","t":"Where pipeline time goes","p":"Before optimising, measure. Split the total time into queue wait, setup, dependency download, build, test and publish. One or two of these usually dominate.","k":[["Why track queue time separately?","It points at runner capacity, which needs a different fix than slow steps."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s18-performance-dora-worked-eks-c2","t":"Caching and incremental work","p":"Reuse work between runs: dependency caches, build caches, Docker layer caches and test result caches. Skip work when nothing relevant changed.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s18-performance-dora-worked-eks-c3","t":"Parallelism, sharding and cancellation","p":"Run independent work at the same time, split big test suites into shards, and cancel runs nobody needs any more.","k":[["Why have a final aggregate job?","Branch protection can require one stable check name instead of every shard."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s18-performance-dora-worked-eks-c4","t":"Cost control","p":"Pipeline cost is minutes times price, plus storage for artifacts and caches. The largest savings come from running less, not from cheaper machines.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s18-performance-dora-worked-eks-c5","t":"The four DORA metrics","p":"DORA research identifies four measures of software delivery performance: deployment frequency, lead time for changes, change failure rate and time to restore service. The last two measure stability, the first two measure throughput.","k":[["Which two metrics measure stability?","Change failure rate and time to restore service."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s18-performance-dora-worked-eks-c6","t":"Measuring DORA from the pipeline","p":"The pipeline and Git already hold most of the data: merge time, deploy time, deploy result and rollback events. A small job or a tool joins them.","k":[["Where do you take change failure data from?","Deployments linked to rollbacks, hotfixes or incidents."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s18-performance-dora-worked-eks-c7","t":"Worked example, the CI half","p":"The service is a containerised Go API deployed to EKS. CI runs on pull request and on merge: test, build, scan, push to ECR by digest, sign, and open a change in the config repository.","k":[["Why does the pull request run skip the push and sign steps?","Untrusted branches should not get registry credentials or produce signed artifacts."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s18-performance-dora-worked-eks-c8","t":"Worked example, the CD half","p":"Deployment is separate: a second workflow updates the image digest in the config repository through a pull request for staging (auto-merged after checks) and production (needs approval). Argo CD then syncs.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s19-gha-advanced-recipes","title":"GitHub Actions advanced recipes","c":[{"id":"s19-gha-advanced-recipes-c1","t":"Path filters for monorepos","p":"A workflow can run only when certain files change, using paths on the trigger or a filter step inside the job.","k":[["Why can a path-filtered required check block merging?","When the workflow is skipped the check never reports, so branch protection waits for it forever."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s19-gha-advanced-recipes-c2","t":"Dynamic matrices","p":"A job can output JSON that a later job uses as its matrix, so the list of things to build comes from the repository rather than the workflow file.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s19-gha-advanced-recipes-c3","t":"Caching and concurrency","p":"Caches keep downloaded dependencies between runs. Concurrency groups stop duplicate runs from piling up.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s19-gha-advanced-recipes-c4","t":"Release automation with release-please or semantic-release","p":"These tools read commit messages and decide the next version, write the changelog and create the GitHub release without human steps.","k":[["Which commit prefix triggers a minor bump?","feat; fix gives a patch and a breaking change gives a major."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s19-gha-advanced-recipes-c5","t":"Reusable workflows with typed inputs and secrets","p":"A reusable workflow is called by other workflows with workflowcall, so one definition serves every repository.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s19-gha-advanced-recipes-c6","t":"Environments, approvals and deployment protection","p":"A GitHub environment attaches approvals, wait timers and secrets to a deploy job.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s19-gha-advanced-recipes-c7","t":"Workflow dispatch, scheduled jobs and manual operations","p":"workflowdispatch lets a person start a workflow from the UI or API with inputs. schedule runs cron jobs.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s19-gha-advanced-recipes-c8","t":"Composite actions and custom actions","p":"A composite action bundles several steps into one reusable step, defined in an action.yml.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s20-jenkins-migration-templates","title":"Jenkins to GitHub Actions and a pipeline template library","c":[{"id":"s20-jenkins-migration-templates-c1","t":"Inventory and migration plan","p":"Before converting anything, list every job, what it does, who owns it and what it needs.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s20-jenkins-migration-templates-c2","t":"Translating a Jenkinsfile","p":"Most declarative constructs have a direct equivalent: stages become jobs, steps become steps, agents become runs-on.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s20-jenkins-migration-templates-c3","t":"Credentials, agents and plugins","p":"The hard parts of migration are secrets, build machines and plugin behaviour.","k":[["Why rotate credentials during migration?","Copying secrets between systems widens exposure and old values may have appeared in logs."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s20-jenkins-migration-templates-c4","t":"Shared libraries to reusable workflows","p":"Jenkins shared libraries hold common pipeline code. In Actions, reusable workflows and composite actions fill that role.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s20-jenkins-migration-templates-c5","t":"Platform template library design","p":"A template library is a set of versioned reusable workflows and actions that encode the organisation's standard pipeline: build, test, scan, sign, deploy.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s20-jenkins-migration-templates-c6","t":"Versioning and governing the library","p":"Consumers need stability. Maintainers need to ship fixes everywhere quickly.","k":[["How does a consumer get a template bug fix without editing files?","They reference a moving major tag such as v3, which maintainers advance for compatible releases."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s20-jenkins-migration-templates-c7","t":"Cutover and rollback checklist","p":"A cutover moves the trigger from Jenkins to Actions for one service and keeps a way back.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s21-pipeline-troubleshooting-release","title":"Pipeline troubleshooting and release engineering","c":[{"id":"s21-pipeline-troubleshooting-release-c1","t":"Method for debugging a failing pipeline","p":"Treat the failure like any incident: find what changed, reproduce it, then fix the cause rather than rerunning.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s21-pipeline-troubleshooting-release-c2","t":"Failure class, flaky tests","p":"A flaky test passes and fails on the same code. Typical causes are timing, shared state, ordering and external services.","k":[["Pass rate of a suite with 20 tests that each flake 1 percent of the time?","0.99 to the 20th is about 0.818, so roughly 18 percent of runs fail."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s21-pipeline-troubleshooting-release-c3","t":"Failure class, cache, dependency and runner problems","p":"Many failures come from the environment around the code: stale caches, unpinned dependencies, full disks and rate limits.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s21-pipeline-troubleshooting-release-c4","t":"Failure class, permissions, OIDC and secrets","p":"Authentication failures are the most common reason a deploy job fails on first use.","k":[["Which permission makes a job able to request an OIDC token?","id-token: write"]],"o":[]},{"id":"s21-pipeline-troubleshooting-release-c5","t":"Failure class, deployment and timeout problems","p":"A deploy job can pass its commands and still leave a broken release, or hang until the timeout.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s21-pipeline-troubleshooting-release-c6","t":"Versioning schemes","p":"A version is a promise about what changed. Semantic Versioning writes it as MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s21-pipeline-troubleshooting-release-c7","t":"Changelogs and release notes","p":"A changelog tells users what changed between versions. It can be generated from Conventional Commit messages.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s21-pipeline-troubleshooting-release-c8","t":"Signing releases and provenance","p":"Signing proves who built an artifact. Provenance records how. Together they let a cluster refuse anything not built by your pipeline.","k":[["Why sign the digest and not the tag?","Tags can be moved to different content; a digest names exactly one image."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s22-database-migration-pipelines","title":"Database migration pipelines with Flyway and Liquibase","c":[{"id":"s22-database-migration-pipelines-c1","t":"Why migrations belong in the pipeline","p":"Schema changes are versioned scripts stored beside the code and applied in order by a tool, not typed into a console.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s22-database-migration-pipelines-c2","t":"Flyway basics","p":"Flyway applies SQL files named V1createtable.sql, V2addindex.sql in version order, and tracks them in the table flywayschemahistory.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s22-database-migration-pipelines-c3","t":"Liquibase basics","p":"Liquibase describes changes as changesets in XML, YAML, JSON or SQL, each identified by id and author, and tracks them in DATABASECHANGELOG.","k":[["What does updateSQL do?","Prints the SQL that update would run, without changing the database, so it can be reviewed."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s22-database-migration-pipelines-c4","t":"Choosing between Flyway and Liquibase","p":"Both work well. Pick on team habits and portability needs.","k":[],"o":[["Flyway","Team knows SQL and uses one database engine","You need engine-neutral definitions"],["Liquibase","Several engines or formal rollback and preconditions","You want the least tooling"]]},{"id":"s22-database-migration-pipelines-c5","t":"Expand and contract for zero downtime","p":"Change the schema in compatible steps so old and new application versions both work during the rollout.","k":[["Why drop the old column in a later release?","Old pods may still run during the first rollout and would fail if the column vanished."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s22-database-migration-pipelines-c6","t":"Running migrations in Kubernetes","p":"Run migrations as a one-off Kubernetes Job or a Helm hook before the new application version, not inside every application pod at start-up.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s22-database-migration-pipelines-c7","t":"Testing, rollback and safety nets","p":"Test migrations against realistic data, and plan how to recover if one fails halfway.","k":[],"o":[]}]}]};